  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nSunday-Next Week:\nAs we progress into Sunday, mid-level heights further increase\nand expand over the entire area. 850mb temperatures push towards\n21 to 24 C, which is highly anomalously for late August as\ndepicted in the EFI/Shift of Tails. This will correlate to\nsurface temperatures in the middle to upper 90s on Sunday\nafternoon, with a few locations towards the Interstate 49\ncorridor pushing 100. Heat index values top out around 100 to\n105, primarily along and west of Highway 65. This pronounced\nheat wave will be characterized by temperatures 10 to 15+\ndegrees above normal for late August, in which average highs are\ntypically in the middle 80s. There is the potential for some\nrecord temperatures as we progress through next week.\n\nThe well above normal temperatures and heat index values up to\n105+ degrees will be accompanied by minimal overnight relief \nnext week with lows in the middle to upper 70s. The extent of \nthe extreme heat is well captured by the NWS HeatRisk product, \nwith widespread Major (3/4) to Extreme (4/4) Heat Risk over the \narea. This outlines the potential for heat- related impacts, \nwith increasing concerns as the prolonged heat envelops the \nentire area next week. At this time, it appears the heat wave \nremains locked in place through at least next Friday or \nSaturday. However, some guidance suggest the heat persists even \nbeyond the seven day forecast period, with WPC and CPC \nhighlighting extreme heat over the central CONUS well into \nearly/mid September. NBM interquartile temperatures spreads \nincrease into next weekend.\n\nCurrently, the highest heat index values of 100 to 105+ degrees\noverspread the area mid next week. With increasing confidence,\nheat headlines will likely be needed as we meet criteria of \ngreater than 100 degree heat index values for 4 or more \nconsecutive days. Check back for updates.\n\nMeanwhile, this period of extreme heat will be dry with no\nmentionable PoPs in the forecast through much of next week. With\nthis in mind, there is even some concerns for fire weather for\nareas west of Highway 65 on Sunday. This area will see breezy\nsouthwest winds in combination with dry and hot weather \nconditions. Of particular concern, will be grass that are dried\nout. The extent and coverage of this heat wave will further \namplify drought conditions over the area, with D1 and D2 now in \neffect for portions of southeast KS and southwest MO. CPC \ncontinues to highlight concerns for a rapid onset drought.",
